          "text": "For Deaf oral histories, access is not a final technical add-on. Language, interpretation, captioning, transcription, camera framing, and interface design affect what can be expressed and what audiences receive. A transcript can support search and quotation, but it does not replace the embodied and linguistic information in a signed or spoken recording. Editors should document who translated or interpreted, preserve the original recording, and avoid forcing culturally specific expression into misleading written equivalents. Accessible presentation and historical accuracy are intertwined."
        },
