        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "A disciplined reading separates three layers. Testimony is what the narrator communicates. Context includes institutions, events, places, and social relationships that make the account legible. Interpretation is the argument a researcher builds by relating the interview to other evidence. Keeping these layers visible prevents an editor from presenting an inference as the narrator's own claim. It also preserves disagreement. Memory can be selective without being useless; the way an event is remembered may itself reveal identity, community, or later change."
        },
